meta {
  name: Check Policy Number
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/policies/check?number=ABC-123-456
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:query {
  number: ABC-123-456
  ~policyType: Auto
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns the check envelope", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("query");
    expect(body).to.have.property("normalized");
    expect(body).to.have.property("matches");
    expect(body.matches).to.be.an("array");
  });

  test("echoes the query verbatim", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().query).to.equal("ABC-123-456");
  });

  test("normalizes punctuation and case away", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().normalized).to.equal("ABC123456");
  });

  test("every match carries the fields needed to recognise the policy", function () {
    // Deliberately tolerant of an empty result: this request runs before any
    // policy has been created (see the run-order note in the docs below), so
    // "no matches" is the expected steady state for a clean database.
    for (const match of res.getBody().matches) {
      expect(match).to.have.property("id");
      expect(match).to.have.property("policyNumber");
      expect(match).to.have.property("policyType");
      expect(match).to.have.property("carrier");
      expect(match).to.have.property("effectiveDate");
      expect(match).to.have.property("isOwn");

      // Identifying fields are withheld for a match outside the caller's scope.
      if (match.isOwn === false) {
        expect(match.clientName).to.equal(null);
        expect(match.householdId).to.equal(null);
        expect(match.dealId).to.equal(null);
      }
    }
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /policies/check — policy-number duplicate check (PAC-40)

  Backs **Card 3** of the Sold wizard. As the producer leaves the policy-number
  field, the form asks whether a policy with that number already exists in the
  agency, so it can offer to **link** rather than create a duplicate.

  There is no legacy equivalent — this contract is net-new.

  - **Module:** `deal_audits` (must be enabled for the agency)
  - **Permission:** `deal_audits:read`
  - **Data scope:** agency-wide lookup, with out-of-scope rows **masked** (see below)

  > **Why `deal_audits` and not `clients`?** The Sold write path is gated on
  > `deal_audits` because that is what a Producer's role template actually
  > grants — it has no `clients:*`, which is where `DealsController` sits.
  > Gating this on a permission producers lack would make the dedupe silently
  > unavailable to the only people who use it. A future *general* Policies read
  > API belongs under `clients` as its own controller.
  >
  > ⚠ Consequence: disabling the `deal_audits` module also disables this check.

  ## Query params
  | param      | type   | required | rules                                        |
  |------------|--------|----------|----------------------------------------------|
  | number     | string | yes      | 1–60 chars; normalized before matching       |
  | policyType | enum   | no       | a canonical `POLICY_TYPES` label; narrows the search |

  ## Matching
  Both the stored key (`Policy.policyNumberKey`) and the query go through the
  same normalizer: **uppercase, strip every non-alphanumeric**. So
  `abc-123-456`, `ABC 123 456` and `abc123456` are one policy.

  A value normalizing to fewer than **4** alphanumerics returns
  `normalized: null` with an empty `matches` — that is "no opinion", not "no
  duplicate". It is **not** a 400: the wizard fires this on every blur,
  including half-typed input, and a validation error there would be noise.

  Test records (`isTestRecord: true`) are never returned. At most **5** matches
  come back — enough to recognise a policy, not to page through a book.

  ## Masking — the one real design call
  A match outside the caller's data scope is **reported, but stripped of
  identifying fields**: `isOwn: false` and `clientName` / `householdId` /
  `dealId` are `null`.

  Hiding those rows entirely would defeat the endpoint. The duplicate a
  producer most needs warning about is precisely the one they cannot see —
  they are about to write a second record for a policy a colleague already
  sold. `policyType`, `carrier` and `effectiveDate` are what make *"is this the
  same policy, or did I mistype?"* answerable, so they are shown; who the
  client is, is not.

  This is deliberately softer than the blanket 404 clamp the write paths use
  (`POST /quote-recaps`, `POST /sold-deals`): those protect a write **target**.

  Ownership is resolved from the match's **deal**, because `Policy` carries no
  `producerId`. A policy with no deal cannot be attributed and is therefore
  masked under `own` scope — mostly migrated rows.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  {
    "query": "ABC-123-456",
    "normalized": "ABC123456",
    "matches": [
      {
        "id": "665f...ac1",
        "policyNumber": "ABC-123-456",
        "policyType": "Auto",
        "carrier": "Allstate",
        "effectiveDate": "2026-01-15T00:00:00.000Z",
        "isOwn": true,
        "clientName": "Dana Dedupe",
        "householdId": "665f...b02",
        "dealId": "665f...c73"
      }
    ]
  }
  ```

  | field         | type           | notes                                              |
  |---------------|----------------|----------------------------------------------------|
  | query         | string         | echoed verbatim, so a stale response can be dropped |
  | normalized    | string \| null | `null` ⇒ input too short; `matches` is then empty   |
  | policyType    | string         | normalized, so migrated raw choice codes read cleanly |
  | effectiveDate | string \| null | ISO-8601                                            |
  | isOwn         | boolean        | `false` ⇒ the three fields below are `null`         |

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — blank/too-long `number`, or a `policyType` outside `POLICY_TYPES`.
  - `401 Unauthorized` — missing/expired access token.
  - `403 Forbidden` — `deal_audits` module disabled, or missing `deal_audits:read`.

  ## Run-order note
  Bruno walks folders alphabetically: `Auth → Deal Audits → Leads → Policies →
  Public Intake → Quote Recaps → Sold Deals`. This folder therefore runs
  **before** any policy has been created, so the tests above assert a
  well-formed envelope rather than a specific hit. The positive-match assertion
  lives in `Sold Deals/`, after the create.
}
